# Git Commits

When creating or suggesting commits:

- Use Conventional Commits: `<type>(optional-scope): <description>`.
- Use lowercase types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `docs`, `chore`, `build`, or `ci`.
- Write in English and use the imperative mood (`add`, `fix`, `remove`).
- Keep the subject concise, preferably at most 72 characters.
- Describe the purpose of the change, not merely the modified files.
- Add a body only when extra context is useful.
- Review the diff and staged files. Stage and commit only changes related to the task; preserve unrelated changes and exclude secrets.
- Run relevant available checks before committing when practical. Report failures honestly; do not claim unexecuted tests passed.
